---
title: "Capture runtime crashes without storing request metadata"
status: "proposed"
---

# Capture runtime crashes without storing request metadata

Design B only. Implementation and deployment wait for the owner's privacy decision.

The invocation-log candidate `270c3b3` remains unchanged on `worker/worker-invocation-logs`. It enables provider request metadata storage and is held.

## Decision

Recommend one private `MischiefTail` Worker with sanitized custom console logs. Keep invocation logs and traces disabled on every producer and consumer.

This adds one Worker resource. The driver can reuse the profile-authenticated telemetry client. Privacy needs a captured-wire proof before this sink is accepted.

| Sink | Persistent data boundary | Qualification limit | Plan footprint |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Consumer console logs | Emit only the safe record; inspect provider enrichment too | Missing coverage or sampled results remain unknown | One Worker create |
| Analytics Engine | Only explicit blobs, numbers and index are written | Sampling occurs on write and read; rare errors can disappear | One Worker create plus a dataset binding |
| Small D1 table | Explicit typed columns with acknowledged inserts | Tail delivery still needs coverage proof | Worker and database create, plus migration |

Alchemy's Analytics Engine dataset is a binding descriptor. It needs no separate API provisioning. That preserves one resource create, but sampling weakens crash qualification.

The current production Stack declares no D1 database. Reusing the database cartridge does not establish an existing production database. A D1 alternative needs two creates here.

**What to notice:** console logs fit the requested footprint. They are acceptable only if the provider adds no upstream request metadata.

## Data boundary

Cloudflare delivers raw `TraceItem` values to the consumer in memory. The consumer persists a newly constructed allowlisted record. It never forwards a TraceItem.

| Field | Source and rule |
| --- | --- |
| Producer script name | Provider-owned `scriptName`; validate its identifier form |
| Producer version | `scriptVersion.id`; optional at the platform boundary |
| Outcome | Allowlisted platform outcome; classify other values without retaining their text |
| Exception name | Allowlisted built-in error class; other names become `UnknownError` |
| Scrubbed message | Constant `[message redacted]`; no raw-message fragments |
| Event time | Valid `eventTimestamp`; retain absence explicitly |
| Exception time | Valid exception timestamp when available |
| Receive time | Consumer clock time |

The record includes no request ID, account ID, URL, headers, cookies, IPs, geo fields, request body or stack trace. It excludes producer console messages and diagnostic-channel payloads.

Consumer failures use constant safe error messages. Decoder inputs and raw Effect causes never enter console output. The privacy property also covers decoder and sink failures.

A message can contain a secret without a recognizable format. Regex replacement cannot prove privacy. Keep the constant placeholder until an approved message grammar exists.

Missing producer version or event time must remain missing. Emit a safe unattributed record. The watch treats such records as unknown when they intersect its producer and window.

## One consumer, three producers

```text
Mischief ----+
Website -----+--> MischiefTail --> safe custom log --> driver watch
RpcBackend --+
```

Use ordinary `tailConsumers: [MischiefTail]` on each producer. Alchemy resolves the resource to its deployed script name and deploys it first.

Do not feed producer resource outputs back into the consumer declaration. That would create a dependency cycle. The consumer reads provider-owned names from TraceItems; the driver resolves its own deployed producer names.

The consumer has no HTTP capability, public route or custom domain. Disable both its Workers development URL and preview URLs. Use the native `workersDev` configuration.

The consumer has no tail consumer of its own. This prevents recursive collection. Keep its invocation logs and traces off; enable only custom logs.

Streaming tails are excluded. Installed beta.80 documents an experimental compatibility flag rejected by production. Local emulation does not prove production delivery.

**What to notice:** completed-invocation tails require less machinery and a supported production path.

## Privacy acceptance gate

1. In an approved isolated stage, send unique canaries in a URL, cookie, IP-like header and request body.
2. Trigger both a handler exception and an initialization exception.
3. Capture the complete consumer telemetry response, including enrichment and failed-consumer events.
4. Prove the safe facts arrived with the producer version and timestamps.
5. Prove no canary or raw upstream request field exists anywhere in the response.
6. Verify all producer and consumer invocation-log settings are off.

The installed Tail event type contains `consumedEvents` with script names. It does not declare upstream headers there. This supports the design but does not prove stored telemetry privacy.

If provider enrichment stores upstream metadata, reject the console sink. Request approval for an explicit D1 sink with a revised two-create plan. Do not silently fall back or enable invocation logs.

## Watch read path

The sink holds derived crash evidence. Cloudflare execution traces remain the source facts. The deploy receipt holds a bounded redacted result, not a log mirror.

Query the consumer's custom records by producer identity, producer version and event-time window. The consumer's `$workers.scriptVersion.id` identifies the consumer version. It cannot identify the producer version.

Capture and validate the provider's custom-field query shape before implementing its adapter. Read unattributed records for the same producer and window too. A version-only filter would hide missing-version failures.

Start the window before the first apply mutation. Query through the watch deadline and include a bounded final delivery grace. Measure that grace in the approved canary stage.

Keep the existing XState watch. Add a declared Effect actor behind a job-shaped crash-read port. Credentials come from the selected Alchemy profile.

- Any matching non-ok record makes the watch red. Recovery cannot erase it.
- Missing coverage, missing identity, incomplete queries or sampled results remain unknown.
- Empty results cannot prove health when producers lack tail attachments or the consumer cannot persist custom logs.
- Successful invocation records provide positive coverage for each watched producer version. Controlled probes must cause those records.
- Query failure and delivery loss cannot become a healthy result.
- The existing approved rollback command remains visible. Rollback remains manual.

Bound each query by the deployment window and producer. Limit returned failure records; one matching failure suffices. Record query completion and sampling evidence. Measure scan cost before choosing polling cadence.

Coverage shows the path worked for observed probes. It does not prove lossless delivery of every invocation. A rollout cannot claim that guarantee without provider evidence.

## Native plan

| Resource | Expected action |
| --- | --- |
| MischiefTail | One create |
| Mischief | Update its tail consumer list |
| Website | Update its tail consumer list |
| RpcBackend | Update its tail consumer list |
| Existing durable resources | Noop |

The exact native plan can also include binding and task rows. Review those separately. Permit only the exact consumer create under the existing plan policy.

No database, dataset resource, public route, network permission or provider credential variable is added. Detaching the consumer is an update. Removing it later is a separate destructive action requiring approval.

## Tests before implementation is accepted

- **Privacy property:** generated request metadata and secret-like exception text cannot appear in any serialized safe record.
- **Schema property:** unknown fields are excluded; malformed versions and timestamps remain explicit unknown facts.
- **History model:** generate old and new versions, window boundaries, duplicates, out-of-order arrivals, missing identities and late delivery.
- **Watch model:** preserve a non-ok result through recovery; empty or sampled reads and unavailable coverage never pass.
- **Terminal-window model:** errors in the last interval are read by the final query; late records beyond the accepted grace remain unknown.
- **Real seam:** captured producer handler and initialization failures reach the private consumer with invocation logs disabled.
- **Wire privacy seam:** inspect the entire consumer response for upstream canaries, including automatic metadata.
- **Plan seam:** compile all three actual declarations; capture one consumer create, three producer updates and expected binding rows without provider mutation.
- **Boundary seam:** no public consumer endpoint, no self-tail edge and no producer-output dependency cycle.
- **Planted bugs:** forwarding a raw TraceItem and querying the consumer version instead of the producer version must fail their properties and models.

Tests are proposed, not implemented. No production qualification is claimed by this design.

